ention the referenced claims.\n\nExplore this article's citation statements on scite.ai\n\n### How to cite\n\nHildebrand, A. (1989), Additive and Multiplicative Functions on Shifted Primes. Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society, s3-59: 209-232. https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-59.2.209--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYu. V. Malykhin, K. S. Ryutin, “Widths and rigidity of unconditional sets and random vectors”, Izv. Math., 89:2 (2025), 261–273 (https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/im9620)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; For $q=1$, the required estimate for the width is contained in Corollary 3.1 to Theorem 3.1 in [1]: $$ \\begin{equation*} d_n^{\\mathrm{avg}}(\\xi,\\ell_1^N) \\geqslant c(\\varepsilon)N,\\qquad n\\leqslant N(1-\\varepsilon). \\end{equation*} \\notag $$ Now let $1<q\\leqslant 2$.\n Proof of Theorem 3. Let $\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_N)$ be a random vector that satisfies $(\\star)$, and $1\\leqslant \\mathsf{E}|\\xi_i|^q< \\infty$ and $i=1,\\dots,N$. For $q=1$, the required estimate for the width is contained in Corollary 3.1 to Theorem 3.1 in [1]: $$ \\begin{equation*} d_n^{\\mathrm{avg}}(\\xi,\\ell_1^N) \\geqslant c(\\varepsilon)N,\\qquad n\\leqslant N(1-\\varepsilon). \\end{equation*} \\notag $$ Now let $1<q\\leqslant 2$. We will prove the following estimate for $n<N$: $$ \\begin{equation} d_n^{\\mathrm{avg}}(\\xi, \\ell_q^N)_q \\geqslant c_q(N-n)^{1/q}. \\end{equation} \\tag{7} $$ The mapping $(x_1,\\dots,x_N)\\mapsto(a_1x_1,\\dots,a_Nx_N)$ is linear and non-expansive for $\\|a\\|_\\infty\\leqslant 1$, and so we can assume that $\\mathsf{E}|\\xi_i|^q=1$ for $i=1,\\dots,N$. We set $\\phi(t):=|t|^{q-1}\\operatorname{sign} t$. Consider the random vector $\\eta$ with coordinates $\\eta_i = \\phi(\\xi_i)-\\mathsf{E}\\phi(\\xi_i)$. It is clear that $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ are biorthogonal: for example, if $\\xi$ has unconditional distribution, then $\\operatornam